# forge-bluetooth.rc — Bluetooth HAL service + local address for the m2note on
# LOS 16.  Source: the m5c file (peripherals lane 2026-09-03).  ONE deliberate
# difference: the service is the stock 64-bit AOSP
# android.hardware.bluetooth@1.0-service — the m2note libbt-vendor.so is built
# from source for both ABIs over libbluetoothdrv.so, which the m2note set has
# in both ABIs (FACT, readelf), so the m5c 32-bit service is not needed.  The
# comments below are the m5c's evidence.  Vendor rc, parsed inside mount_all.
#
# 1. The service.  Replaces the 64-bit AOSP android.hardware.bluetooth@1.0-
#    service placed on 2026-08-29: that one registers fine (btHci != nullptr
#    stopped aborting), but its passthrough impl cannot dlopen libbt-vendor.so
#    — this device's blob set has it 32-bit only — so the first BT enable
#    would fail.  See bluetooth_hal/Android.bp.  Same service name as the AOSP
#    rc (vendor.bluetooth-1-0) so the framework-side ctl.* and the vts hooks
#    keep working; `override` because both rc files may be present and init
#    keeps the first definition otherwise.
#
#    Groups: the AOSP rc has only `bluetooth`.  libbluetooth_mtk reads the
#    NVRAM BD address record through libnvram in THIS process and programs the
#    controller with it (14.1 M5C_BT_ADDR_LANE.md: vendor HCI 0xFC1A,
#    radiomod.c:291; the address the stack passes is ignored).  The record is
#    root:3008 0660 from stock; AID 3008 (net_bt_stack) no longer exists on
#    Pie, hence the chown below (m95 shape), plus the wider group list of the
#    vendor/mediatek .mtk rc (net_bt_stack there is load-bearing on m681).
#
# 2. The address.  Pie's HAL (hardware/interfaces/bluetooth/1.0/default/
#    bluetooth_address.cc) reads the local address from exactly three sources
#    — the file named by ro.bt.bdaddr_path, ro.boot.btmacaddr,
#    persist.service.bdroid.bdaddr — and vendor_interface.cc:212 does
#    LOG_ALWAYS_FATAL("No Bluetooth Address!") when all miss.  On this image
#    all three are unset (FACT, getprop 2026-09-03); the 14.1 tree published
#    the address via btaddr_mtk -> /data/misc/bluetooth/bdaddr + ro.bt.bdaddr_path,
#    which LOS 16 does not carry.  m5c-bdaddr.sh (port of m95-bdaddr.sh) reads
#    the six factory bytes — d8 6c 02 ab 6f 3f on this unit, = 14.1's
#    <factory-address-redacted> — and publishes persist.service.bdroid.bdaddr.  The HAL
#    reads it at BT enable, not at boot, so post-fs-data is early enough.

#
# 3. /dev/stpbt.  ueventd.mt6735.rc (ramdisk) says `0660 bluetooth net_bt_stack`;
#    on Pie that group name does not resolve, so ueventd leaves the node at
#    root:root 0600 (FACT, ls -l on the live LOS 16, 2026-09-03) and the HAL
#    (user bluetooth) cannot open it.  Fixed in the tree's ueventd too, but
#    that only lands with the next boot.img; this chown covers the current one.

on post-fs-data
    chown bluetooth bluetooth /dev/stpbt
    chmod 0660 /dev/stpbt
    chown root bluetooth /data/nvram/APCFG/APRDEB/BT_Addr
    chmod 0660 /data/nvram/APCFG/APRDEB/BT_Addr
    exec_background - root bluetooth -- /vendor/bin/sh /vendor/bin/m2note-bdaddr.sh

# forge m5c (2026-09-03, bt-stp): the chown at post-fs-data above CANNOT work.
# /dev/stpbt does not exist yet at that point.  The node appears only when
# wmt_loader loads the connectivity drivers ("[MTK-BT] BT_init: mtk_stp_BT_chrdev
# driver (major 192) installed"), and wmtLoader is `class core`, started by
# `class_start core` in init.rc's `on boot` (init.rc:677) - long after
# `on post-fs-data` (init.rc:389).  FACT from the 14:10 boot: the node came up
# root:root 0600, the HAL (user bluetooth) could not open it and BT crash-looped
# ("Disabled due to CRASH" every ~2 s) even with the transport fix in place; a
# manual chown made it enable immediately.  Same shape as the port's other
# ordering traps (prop.default, fw_path[], import /init.${ro.zygote}.rc): an
# early action touching something created later.
#
# Redo the chown once the oneshot wmtLoader has finished (the node exists by
# then; conn_launcher, same class, starts after it), and once more at
# boot_completed to cover the ueventd/uevent race.  The permanent fix is the
# tree's ueventd.mt6735.rc (`bluetooth bluetooth`), which needs a new boot.img.
# Backstop.  Both triggers above can miss: the wmtLoader one races ueventd's
# asynchronous node creation, and sys.boot_completed is NOT dependable on this
# build - observed EMPTY on the 14:10 boot with zygote running and system_server
# answering dumpsys, i.e. the framework can be up while the property never gets
# set.  m5c-stpbt-perm.sh waits for the node (up to 60 s) and then chowns it,
# and prints the outcome to /dev/kmsg so the next boot's dmesg says plainly
# whether it fired.  exec_background is the same mechanism m5c-bdaddr.sh above
# already uses successfully on this device.

on property:init.svc.wmtLoader=stopped
    chown bluetooth bluetooth /dev/stpbt
    chmod 0660 /dev/stpbt
    exec_background - root root -- /vendor/bin/sh /vendor/bin/m2note-stpbt-perm.sh

on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    chown bluetooth bluetooth /dev/stpbt
    chmod 0660 /dev/stpbt

service vendor.bluetooth-1-0 /vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.bluetooth@1.0-service
    override
    class hal
    capabilities BLOCK_SUSPEND NET_ADMIN SYS_NICE
    user bluetooth
    group bluetooth radio net_bt net_bt_admin inet system
    writepid /dev/stune/foreground/tasks
